WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.240
Say you've recorded an order request with
its routing mode set to live.

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:07.120
Your test environment expects sandbox.

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:11.920
A transform lets Speedscale make that
change when it replays the request.

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:15.440
The original recording is still there for
you to inspect.

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:17.840
Let's change just that one field.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:20.960
Open Prepare, select Tests, and click New.

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:25.120
Tests is the side that sends requests to
the app you're testing.

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:29.600
Mocks is where you'd tune the traffic used
to stand in for its dependencies.

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:33.980
First, set Runs on. That's the filter for
which requests get this rule.

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:37.600
Choose Endpoint, then select the recorded
orders endpoint.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:40.980
This keeps the rule focused on the orders
endpoint.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:45.460
If nothing matches, check the endpoint and
which side you selected.

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:48.680
Next, click Pull from and choose Request
Body.

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:51.420
That's the source of the value we'll work
with.

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:56.340
Our routing mode is inside the JSON body,
so the first step will pick it out.

00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:59.260
Click Add step and choose JSON Path.

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:02.360
Enter routing.mode, then click Done.

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:04.480
That gives us just the routing mode.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:06.840
Add a second step called Constant.

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:09.200
Enter sandbox and click Done.

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:11.280
The first step finds the field.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:13.720
The second supplies the replacement.

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:17.480
Pick a request in Matching requests and
read down the chain.

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:21.720
We pull the body, find live, and replace
it with sandbox.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:26.160
Under Write back to, Replay uses shows the
full JSON body,

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:30.160
including the order ID, with just the
routing mode changed.

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:32.660
Check a second request the same way.

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:36.160
When the preview looks right, click Save
and reanalyze.

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:38.660
That saves the draft to this snapshot.

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:42.660
Once analysis completes, return to Traffic
and open

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:46.160
Compare to see the original request
alongside the request

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:48.660
with the saved transform applied.

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:52.160
Finish with a small replay against your
test environment.

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:55.660
Open the report and check that the order
requests were sent

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:57.780
and passed the expected checks.

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:00.980
The preview told us what value the rule
would write.

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:04.140
The replay tells us how the application
responded.
